---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../../data/site';

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Business Mailing Lists',
  provider: {
    '@type': 'LocalBusiness',
    '@id': 'https://crst.net/#organization',
    name: BUSINESS.name,
    telephone: BUSINESS.phone,
  },
  url: 'https://crst.net/services/business-mailing-lists',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Business Mailing Lists — B2B Targeting by Industry & Size"
  description="Target businesses by industry, employee count, revenue, and location. How to build B2B mailing lists that drive qualified leads."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  </Fragment>
  <PageHero
    title="Business Mailing Lists"
    subtitle="Most business mailing lists are too broad to work. Here's how to filter down to companies that actually need what you're selling."
    breadcrumbs={[
      { label: 'Services', href: '/services' },
      { label: 'Business Mailing Lists' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="py-14 lg:py-20">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-10 lg:gap-14">
        <div class="lg:col-span-2 prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <div class="section-divider mb-6"></div>

          <p class="text-lg text-navy-700 leading-relaxed font-semibold mb-6">
            You buy a list of "all HVAC contractors in the Hudson Valley." It comes back with 847 companies. You mail all of them. Half are residential-only operations with 1-2 employees who can't afford your equipment. A quarter went out of business. Another chunk are national franchises that buy through corporate procurement.
          </p>

          <p class="text-navy-600/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
            You just spent $2,400 on printing and postage to reach maybe 200 actual prospects. Business mailing lists only work when they're filtered correctly. Industry code isn't enough. You need employee count, revenue range, years in business, and decision-maker titles.
          </p>

          <p class="text-navy-600/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
            We've been building B2B mailing lists for Hudson Valley businesses since 1998. We know the difference between a list that looks good on paper and a list that actually drives meetings.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-8 border-navy-200" />

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-display text-crst-primary mb-4">Why Business Mailing Lists Still Work</h2>

          <p class="text-navy-600/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            B2B direct mail has a reputation problem. Everyone assumes email and LinkedIn are the only ways to reach business buyers. But the data says otherwise:
          </p>

          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-navy-600/80 space-y-2 mb-8">
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Higher open rates than email:</strong> Physical mail 70–90% vs. email 15–25%</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Longer shelf life:</strong> Mail sits on desks for days; email gets deleted in seconds</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Better recall:</strong> 73% of B2B decision-makers remember receiving mail vs. 44% for digital ads</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-navy-600/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
            The reason most B2B mail fails isn't the channel — it's the list. What doesn't work: mailing every business in an industry code with no filters. What works: filtering by industry + size + revenue + geography.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-8 border-navy-200" />

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-display text-crst-primary mb-6">How to Target Business Mailing Lists</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 mb-8">
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">1. Industry Classification (SIC/NAICS Codes)</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">SIC and NAICS are codes that categorize what a business does. The mistake: using only a 2-digit code. NAICS 23 is "Construction" — but that includes framers, general contractors, and heavy civil engineering. They don't all buy the same things. Fix: Use 4-digit or 6-digit codes to narrow to the exact type of business.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">2. Employee Count</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">The best proxy for company size and buying capacity. A 3-person dental practice has different needs than a 40-person group practice. For most B2B campaigns, target 5+ employees. Businesses under 5 are often too small to have predictable budgets.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">3. Annual Revenue</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">Indicates financial capacity and buying power better than employee count alone. A 10-person business doing $500K in revenue operates differently than a 10-person business doing $5M. For most B2B services and equipment sales, target $1M+ in revenue.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">4. Years in Business</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">Brand-new businesses (0-2 years) are often strapped for cash. Established businesses (5+ years) have predictable cash flow and are more likely to invest. For most B2B campaigns, target 3+ years in business.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">5. Geographic Boundaries</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">If you only serve a three-county area, mailing businesses in Albany is pointless. Filter by radius, county, ZIP codes, or state depending on your service area.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">6. Decision-Maker Titles</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">Mailing "Acme Construction Co." isn't as effective as mailing "John Smith, VP of Operations, Acme Construction." Contact-level lists cost more but reach the actual decision-maker.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-8 border-navy-200" />

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-display text-crst-primary mb-6">Industries That Respond to B2B Mail</h2>

          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-navy-600/80 space-y-3 mb-8">
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Insurance Agencies:</strong> Target small businesses by employee count and industry</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Commercial Contractors:</strong> HVAC, plumbing, electrical targeting facility managers and business owners</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Professional Services:</strong> Accounting, legal, IT, HR targeting businesses 5-50 employees</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Equipment & Supply Vendors:</strong> Kitchen equipment for restaurants, medical supplies for practices</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Nonprofits:</strong> Filtered by budget size and mission type</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-8 border-navy-200" />

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-display text-crst-primary mb-6">Common B2B List Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 mb-8">
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">Using Only Industry Code with No Other Filters</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">You buy "all dental practices." The list includes solo practitioners, large group practices, and specialty practices that don't fit your offer. Fix: Layer on employee count, revenue, years in business, and specialty codes.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">Mailing Businesses Too Small to Buy</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">You sell commercial-grade equipment starting at $15K and mail every business, including 1-2 person operations doing $200K in revenue. They can't afford what you're selling. Fix: Set minimums on employee count and revenue.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">Not Cleaning for Duplicates and Multi-Location Issues</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">Your list includes the same agency listed three times, merged businesses, and franchise locations reporting to corporate. Fix: Deduplicate by company name and EIN. Exclude franchise chains if targeting independent decision-makers.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 mb-2">Ignoring Data Decay</h3>
              <p class="text-navy-600/70 leading-relaxed">You buy a business list and use it six months later. In that time, 3-5% of businesses moved or closed, contacts changed, and phone numbers went stale. Fix: Re-process lists every 90 days if using them repeatedly.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="my-8 border-navy-200" />

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-display text-crst-primary mb-4">B2B List Pricing</h2>

          <ul class="list-none text-navy-600/80 space-y-2 mb-8">
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Company-only lists:</strong> $125–$200 per 1,000 records</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Contact-level lists (with decision-maker names):</strong> $150–$250 per 1,000 records</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-800">Enhanced data (email, phone, LinkedIn):</strong> $200–$350 per 1,000 records</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-navy-600/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
            What's included: NCOA processing, CASS certification, deduplication, presort, and file formatting. You're not paying extra for clean data — that's just how we do it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-crst-primary/5 border border-crst-primary/15 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10 text-center">
            <p class="font-heading font-bold text-crst-primary text-lg mb-2">Build Your Business List</p>
            <p class="text-navy-600/70 text-sm mb-4">Tell us the industry, size, and geography. We'll provide a count and quote.</p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-3">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary text-sm">Get a Free Estimate</a>
              <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-heading font-semibold text-crst-primary hover:text-crst-accent transition-colors">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M3 5a2 2 0 012-2h3.28a1 1 0 01.948.684l1.498 4.493a1 1 0 01-.502 1.21l-2.257 1.13a11.042 11.042 0 005.516 5.516l1.13-2.257a1 1 0 011.21-.502l4.493 1.498a1 1 0 01.684.949V19a2 2 0 01-2 2h-1C9.716 21 3 14.284 3 6V5z"/></svg>
                Call {BUSINESS.phone}
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <div class="lg:col-span-1">
          <div class="sticky top-24 space-y-5">
            <div class="bg-crst-primary rounded-xl p-6 text-white">
              <h3 class="font-display text-lg mb-2">Ready to Target Companies?</h3>
              <p class="text-white/60 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">Tell us who you want to reach. We'll provide a count and quote — usually within a few hours.</p>
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-accent w-full text-center text-sm">Request a List Quote</a>
              <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10 text-center">
                <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="text-white/70 text-sm font-heading hover:text-white transition-colors">
                  or call {BUSINESS.phone}
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-navy-50/50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-navy-100">
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 text-sm mb-3">Related Services</h3>
              <div class="space-y-2">
                {[
                  { label: 'Mailing Lists & Data Services', href: '/services/data-lists' },
                  { label: 'Direct Mail Lists', href: '/services/direct-mail-lists' },
                  { label: 'Consumer Mailing Lists', href: '/services/consumer-mailing-lists' },
                  { label: 'Direct Mail Campaigns', href: '/services/direct-mail' },
                ].map((link) => (
                  <a href={link.href} class="flex items-center justify-between px-3 py-2 text-sm text-navy-700 hover:text-crst-primary hover:bg-white rounded-md transition-colors font-heading">
                    {link.label}
                    <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 opacity-40" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
                  </a>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-navy-50/50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-navy-100">
              <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-navy-800 text-sm mb-3">Data Sources</h3>
              <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-navy-600/70">
                <p><strong class="text-navy-800">Dun & Bradstreet</strong> and Experian Business for most company data</p>
                <p><strong class="text-navy-800">Contact-level data</strong> from multiple compilers for accuracy</p>
                <p><strong class="text-navy-800">Multi-source verification</strong> to ensure data quality</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection />
</BaseLayout>
